## REASONING

**Decomposition.** The request has one explicit goal — "reproduce the daily streamflow hydrograph at the American River gauge and report NSE" — but that goal hides two requirements the single-column stack cannot meet on its own: (a) generating runoff over the *contributing area* above the gauge, and (b) *routing/aggregating* that runoff to a hydrograph at a point. ELM single-column produces per-column water partitioning (surface runoff QOVER, sub-surface drainage QDRAI) but has **no river routing and no spatial aggregation**. A single column, or even a stratified set of columns, yields local runoff *generation*, not gauged discharge. So NSE-against-daily-streamflow is not achievable as posed.

**Physical setting.** Naches spans 352–1977 m — a snow-dominated montane basin. The daily hydrograph here is governed by **elevation-banded snow accumulation/melt timing**. Reproducing it demands elevation-downscaled forcing (lapse rates on T and precip) and snowpack dynamics resolved by band. The brief lists only "forcing" as a candidate driver and gives a *uniform* forcing archive (1980–2018) with no downscaling capability. Columns stratified by elevation under uniform forcing would vary by soil, not by the climate/snow gradient that actually shapes the hydrograph — so the dominant mechanism is not representable.

**Verdict: partial → effectively infeasible for the primary ask.** What the runs *can* do: characterize how runoff generation (partitioning between QOVER and QDRAI, snowmelt timing) varies across the basin's elevation/soil strata, as a physically-grounded input to a future routing experiment. What they *cannot* do: produce a routed daily discharge time series at the gauge or a defensible NSE.

**Design.** I keep the sampling honest: a small elevation × soil stratification to span runoff-generation heterogeneity of the contributing area (exploratory), and I pin the one validation asset (the gauge) but label it as **aggregated/routed — currently unsupported**, not co-located. N is kept small (spanning 3 elevation bands × 2–3 dominant soils) because without routing, more columns do not buy a better hydrograph.

